  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1154 PM CDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nEarly next week, a weak, cutoff low is still being analyzed in \nlonger range CAMs and global models, however, it is now tracking too \nfar west to keep the Panhandles in a favorable location for \nprecipitation. Despite better moisture advection during this period, \nthe westward shift lowers confidence in precipitation occurring \nacross the Panhandles, which is reflected in our PoPs.\n\nLate next work week, long range models are still deciphering what \nour next weather system will do. As it stands, a deep trough will \nenter the western US and track eastward. Operational models favor \nour region for potential precipitation, however, there are several \ndiscrepancies between them. Some show the trough not being as deep, \nand keeps positive vorticity maxima and the best moisture outside of \nthe Panhandles. Others depict the Panhandles in the ideal location \nof both positive voriticity maxima and moisture, leading to a \nstronger signal of precipitation. Ensemble guidance has medium \nchances of at least 0.1\" QPF at this time. However, high uncertainty \nremains in system timing and impact magnitude given the extended \nforecast lead time. As such, we will be watching to see how models \nresolve the trough in the coming days and adjust the forecast as \nneeded. \n\nAbdullayev/Vanden Bosch",
